{
  "version": "0.6.3",
  "notes": "Found the actual root cause of the data loss (0.6.1/0.6.2 were treating the symptom, not the cause): on a real run, exactly 160 characters vanished from the middle of a single 1024-byte HTTPREAD chunk -- not at a chunk boundary at all, and a different amount than the 1024 bytes lost the time before. Random amount, random position, never reproducible in pure-string simulation -- classic UART receive-buffer overflow: MicroPython's default UART rxbuf is far smaller than a multi-KB burst the modem sends back-to-back for a chunked response, so excess bytes get silently dropped at the driver level before Python ever sees them. Fix: explicit rxbuf=4096 on the UART constructor (Sim7600Modem.RX_BUFFER_SIZE), plus a tighter poll interval in read_until() (10ms instead of 50ms) as defense in depth for future larger responses. The chunk-aware parser and expected_length validation from 0.6.1/0.6.2 stay in place as a safety net in case a response ever does exceed the buffer.",
